  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{\u2218}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{\u2218}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{\u2218}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({135}^{\u2218})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
